  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1137 PM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nTonight \n\nA shortwave trough will move southeast along the US/Canadian\nborder tonight. Soundings show that the air mass over the area \nis very dry below 20K feet. It is interesting that the soundings\nshow that the air mass between 500 and 300 mb has lapse rates \nover 8C/km. This could result in some virga well aloft. Low \ntemperatures will range from the lower 30s to lower 40s.\n\nSunday through Tuesday \n\nHigh pressure will provide dry weather through this period. As\nthe winds become more southerly with time, temperatures will\ngradually warm. High temperatures will be in the 50s on Sunday\nand Tuesday and range from the mid-50s to lower 60s on Monday.\nLow temperatures will range from the mid-20s to mid-30s Sunday\nnight and be in the 30s and 40s for Monday and Tuesday nights.\n\nWednesday into Friday\n\nA Canadian cold front will move southeast through the area on\nWednesday afternoon/night. This might bring some small chances\nof accumulating precipitation to the Midwest, but confidence was\ntoo low to include in the forecast at this time. \n\nAs the long-wave upper level ridge amplifies to the west from\nThursday into Friday, a long-wave trough will develop along and\neast of the Mississippi River. The coldest air continues to \nlook to be over New England and the eastern Great Lakes.\n\nHigh temperatures will be in the 40s and 50s on Wednesday and in\nthe 40s for Thursday and Friday. Low temperatures will be in \nthe 30s on Wednesday and range from the mid-20s to mid-30s on \nThursday and Friday nights.",
